1. A method for estimating a distance between a vehicle and an electronic identifier, the method comprising:

synchronizing a time base or measurement reference of the vehicle with a corresponding time base or measurement reference of the electronic identifier;

selecting a plurality of frequencies comprising N selected frequencies;

for each selected frequency of the plurality of frequencies:

transmitting, by the vehicle, a radio-frequency signal at the selected frequency to the electronic identifier;

measuring, by the electronic identifier, a phase of the radio-frequency signal received from the vehicle at the selected frequency;

transmitting, by the electronic identifier, a return radio-frequency signal at the same selected frequency to the vehicle;

measuring, by the vehicle, a phase of the return radio-frequency signal received from the electronic identifier at the selected frequency; and

determining, for the selected frequency, an averaged phase based on the phase measured by the electronic identifier and the phase measured by the vehicle;

computing phase slopes between averaged phases corresponding to adjacent selected frequencies; and

estimating the distance between the vehicle and the electronic identifier based at least in part on the computed phase slopes.

2. The method of claim 1, wherein the electronic identifier comprises a key fob, portable access device, credential, transponder, mobile device, or vehicle access token.

3. The method of claim 1, wherein synchronizing the vehicle and the electronic identifier comprises establishing a common phase reference, timing reference, frequency reference, clock relationship, or calibration relationship.

4. The method of claim 1, wherein the averaged phase for a selected frequency is determined by computing an arithmetic mean of the phase measured by the electronic identifier and the phase measured by the vehicle.

5. The method of claim 1, wherein computing the phase slopes comprises determining, for each pair of adjacent selected frequencies, a difference between respective averaged phases divided by a difference between the corresponding adjacent selected frequencies.

6. The method of claim 1, wherein estimating the distance comprises converting at least one of the computed phase slopes into a propagation delay and converting the propagation delay into the distance.

7. The method of claim 1, wherein estimating the distance comprises combining a plurality of the computed phase slopes to obtain a refined distance estimate.

8. The method of claim 7, wherein combining the plurality of computed phase slopes comprises averaging, filtering, weighting, rejecting outliers, or applying a statistical estimator.

9. The method of claim 1, wherein the N selected frequencies are selected within an ultra-wideband, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, near-field, radio-frequency identification, or other radio-frequency communication band.

10. The method of claim 1, wherein the selected frequencies are non-uniformly spaced.

11. The method of claim 1, wherein the selected frequencies are uniformly spaced.

12. The method of claim 1, further comprising unwrapping the averaged phases prior to computing the phase slopes.

13. The method of claim 1, further comprising compensating the measured phases for at least one of antenna phase response, transmitter phase offset, receiver phase offset, cable delay, processing delay, oscillator offset, or calibration error.

14. The method of claim 1, wherein the distance is estimated for a passive entry, passive start, vehicle access, vehicle immobilizer, or vehicle authorization function.

15. A vehicle system comprising:

a vehicle radio-frequency transceiver configured to transmit signals at a plurality of selected frequencies and receive return signals at the plurality of selected frequencies;

a synchronization module configured to synchronize a measurement reference of the vehicle system with a measurement reference of an electronic identifier; and

one or more processors configured to:

cause the vehicle radio-frequency transceiver to transmit, for each of N selected frequencies, a signal to the electronic identifier;

obtain, for each selected frequency, a first phase measurement made by the electronic identifier from the signal received at the electronic identifier;

obtain, for each selected frequency, a second phase measurement made by the vehicle radio-frequency transceiver from a same-frequency return signal received from the electronic identifier;

determine, for each selected frequency, an averaged phase based on the first phase measurement and the second phase measurement;

compute slopes between averaged phases for adjacent selected frequencies; and

estimate a distance between the vehicle system and the electronic identifier based at least in part on the computed slopes.

16. The vehicle system of claim 15, wherein the vehicle radio-frequency transceiver is further configured to communicate phase measurement data with the electronic identifier.

17. The vehicle system of claim 15, wherein the one or more processors are further configured to determine whether the electronic identifier is within an authorized distance threshold based on the estimated distance.

18. The vehicle system of claim 17, wherein the one or more processors are further configured to enable or inhibit at least one vehicle function based on whether the electronic identifier is within the authorized distance threshold.

19. An electronic identifier comprising:

a radio-frequency transceiver configured to receive a vehicle signal at each of a plurality of selected frequencies and to transmit a return signal at the same frequency as the received vehicle signal;

a synchronization module configured to synchronize a measurement reference of the electronic identifier with a measurement reference of a vehicle; and

one or more processors configured to:

measure, for each of N selected frequencies, a phase of the vehicle signal received from the vehicle;

cause the radio-frequency transceiver to transmit, for each selected frequency, the return signal at the same selected frequency to the vehicle; and

communicate phase measurement information to the vehicle to enable the vehicle to determine, for each selected frequency, an averaged phase based on the phase measured by the electronic identifier and a phase measured by the vehicle, compute phase slopes between adjacent selected frequencies, and estimate a distance between the vehicle and the electronic identifier.

20. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a vehicle system, cause the vehicle system to perform operations comprising:

synchronizing with an electronic identifier;

transmitting a signal at each of N selected frequencies to the electronic identifier;

receiving, from the electronic identifier, phase measurement information corresponding to phases measured by the electronic identifier for the N selected frequencies;

receiving, from the electronic identifier, return signals at the same N selected frequencies;

measuring phases of the return signals at the N selected frequencies;

determining averaged phases for the N selected frequencies using the phases measured by the electronic identifier and the phases measured by the vehicle system;

computing phase slopes between averaged phases corresponding to adjacent selected frequencies; and

estimating a distance between the vehicle system and the electronic identifier based on the computed phase slopes.

1. A method for measuring a distance (R) between a vehicle (10) and an access badge (20), the method comprising:

- performing, between the vehicle (10) and the access badge (20), a plurality of frequency sequences (S1, S2, …, Sn), each frequency sequence comprising transmission of radio-frequency signals at a plurality of frequencies (f1, f2, …, fm);

- for each frequency (fi) of each frequency sequence (Sj), measuring, at the access badge (20), a first phase value (φB,i,j) of a signal received from the vehicle (10), the first phase value (φB,i,j) being determined modulo 2π;

- for each frequency (fi) of each frequency sequence (Sj), measuring, at the vehicle (10), a second phase value (φV,i,j) of a signal received from the access badge (20), the second phase value (φV,i,j) being determined modulo 2π;

- determining, for each frequency (fi), an average first phase value (φ̄B,i) from the first phase values (φB,i,j) obtained over the plurality of frequency sequences (S1, S2, …, Sn);

- determining, for each frequency (fi), an average second phase value (φ̄V,i) from the second phase values (φV,i,j) obtained over the plurality of frequency sequences (S1, S2, …, Sn);

- calculating a first phase ramp (RB) from the average first phase values (φ̄B,i) as a function of the corresponding frequencies (fi);

- calculating a second phase ramp (RV) from the average second phase values (φ̄V,i) as a function of the corresponding frequencies (fi); and

- calculating the distance (R) between the vehicle (10) and the access badge (20) from the first phase ramp (RB) and the second phase ramp (RV).
